## 9. DSA, OOP & DBMS considerations

### OOP
Use classes and appropriate object-oriented principles to represent users, profiles, opportunities, projects, and requests. Role-specific profiles should contain information and operations relevant to each role.

### DSA
Develop suitable searching, matching, sorting, and ranking logic for opportunities and mentors. Select data structures based on actual operations and explain their time and space complexity. The final algorithms and restrictions on built-in Java functions must be agreed with the supervisor.

### DBMS
Use an SQL database to store user accounts, profiles, skills, opportunities, research projects, applications, and requests. The detailed relational schema, keys, constraints, and relationships will be designed during the database-design phase.

